"use strict";(self.webpackChunkbast_docu=self.webpackChunkbast_docu||[]).push([[235],{88817:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"/2026/07/08/beta-26.3","metadata":{"permalink":"/dadadocu/dadaprogressions-changelog/2026/07/08/beta-26.3","source":"@site/dadaprogressions-changelog/2026-07-08-beta-26.3.md","title":"DadaProgressions Beta 26.3","description":"This update finishes the point-rule workflow. The calculation system was already flexible, but configuring and checking a long rule list still meant spending too much time in YAML. Beta 26.3 makes that part visible in the editor and much easier to diagnose on a live server.","date":"2026-07-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"DadaProgressions Beta 26.1","permalink":"/dadadocu/dadaprogressions-changelog/beta-26.1"}},"content":"This update finishes the point-rule workflow. The calculation system was already flexible, but configuring and checking a long rule list still meant spending too much time in YAML. Beta 26.3 makes that part visible in the editor and much easier to diagnose on a live server.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Point rules\\n\\nEvery point rule can now choose how its own conditions are combined:\\n\\n```yaml\\npoints:\\n  base: 1\\n  rules:\\n    - when-mode: ALL\\n      when:\\n        material: [DIAMOND_ORE]\\n        world: [survival]\\n      set: 10\\n```\\n\\n`ALL` requires every condition in that rule. `ANY` accepts it as soon as one condition passes. This setting is local to the point rule; the goal or criterion keeps its own independent `filter-mode`.\\n\\nRules are still evaluated from top to bottom and the first match wins. If none match, the configured base amount is left unchanged.\\n\\n## Visual editor\\n\\nThe criterion editor now has a dedicated **Point rules** screen. From there an administrator can:\\n\\n- choose `EVENT_AMOUNT` or a fixed base;\\n- change rounding;\\n- add `SET` and `MULTIPLY` rules;\\n- edit values and `ALL`/`ANY` matching;\\n- reorder or remove rules;\\n- enter the rule conditions through private chat.\\n\\nCondition input uses a compact form such as:\\n\\n```text\\nmaterial=DIAMOND_ORE,DEEPSLATE_DIAMOND_ORE; world.exclude=lobby\\n```\\n\\nAs with the rest of the goal editor, a change is validated before it becomes active. An invalid edit is rolled back instead of leaving half-written configuration behind.\\n\\n## Better event debugging\\n\\nThe one-shot debug command now reports the winning point rule, its operation and matching mode, followed by the base-to-final point calculation:\\n\\n```text\\n/dp admin debug <goalId> <onlinePlayer> [timeoutSeconds]\\n```\\n\\nThe default timeout is 60 seconds and can be set from 5 to 600 seconds. A pending trace can be removed with:\\n\\n```text\\n/dp admin debug cancel <onlinePlayer>\\n```\\n\\nThis makes it much clearer whether an event was rejected by the goal filters, accepted by a point rule, blocked by a prerequisite, or stopped by a cooldown.\\n\\n## YAML assistance\\n\\nA JSON Schema is now included for goal files. Compatible editors can suggest keys and enum values and flag common structural mistakes while the file is being written. Server-side `/dp admin validate` is still the final check, since it also knows about the installed Bukkit version and the other goal files.\\n\\n## Compatibility\\n\\nExisting point rules do not need to be changed. A rule without `when-mode` continues to behave as `ALL`, which is the same matching behavior used before this release."},{"id":"beta-26.1","metadata":{"permalink":"/dadadocu/dadaprogressions-changelog/beta-26.1","source":"@site/dadaprogressions-changelog/2026-07-01-beta-26.1.md","title":"DadaProgressions Beta 26.1","description":"Beta 26.1 makes goals much more flexible and improves how progress notifications are delivered during busy sessions.","date":"2026-07-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"dadaprogressions","permalink":"/dadadocu/dadaprogressions-changelog/tags/dadaprogressions"}],"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"mrbast","title":"DadaProgressions Developer","url":"https://www.spigotmc.org/members/mrbast.610454/","key":"mrbast","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"beta-26.1","title":"DadaProgressions Beta 26.1","authors":["mrbast"],"tags":["dadaprogressions"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"DadaProgressions Beta 26.3","permalink":"/dadadocu/dadaprogressions-changelog/2026/07/08/beta-26.3"}},"content":"Beta 26.1 makes goals much more flexible and improves how progress notifications are delivered during busy sessions.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Modular goals\\n\\nGoals can now contain multiple criteria joined with `AND` or `OR`. Each criterion keeps its own progress, so a goal can ask players to complete several different tasks or let them choose between alternatives.\\n\\nFilters are available for materials, entities, worlds, game modes, permissions, commands, damage causes, and player attribution. Point rules can also assign fixed values or multipliers to specific actions.\\n\\nExisting single-type goals continue to work without changes.\\n\\n## Goal chains\\n\\nGoals can depend on other goals. A prerequisite may require the player to have completed a goal during the current period or at any point in the past.\\n\\nInvalid chains, missing prerequisites, and circular dependencies are reported when goals are loaded.\\n\\n## New community goal examples\\n\\nThe default configuration now includes four examples built with the modular goal engine:\\n\\n- `community_daily_supply_run`\\n- `community_weekly_beast_cleanup`\\n- `community_weekly_breeding_festival`\\n- `community_monthly_monument_project`\\n\\nAnimal breeding is now tracked automatically, including attribution for older server versions.\\n\\n## Clearer goal list\\n\\nGoal entries now show a short description of the actual objective. Their lore supports live progress and target placeholders, while the more technical details remain in the individual goal view.\\n\\nExtended state lore is hidden by default. It can be restored with `gui.list.goal-item.show-state-lore`.\\n\\n## Notification queues\\n\\nChat, action bar, title, and boss bar notifications now use separate queues. Repeated updates for the same goal are combined, preserving the starting value and total progress gained instead of filling the screen with one message per action.\\n\\nQueue timing, size, and channel limits can be configured globally, for a specific event, or for an individual goal.\\n\\n## Chat batching\\n\\nUpdates from several goals can be grouped into a single chat message while keeping their original order. The separator and maximum number of entries are configurable; setting `chat-batch.max-entries` to `0` includes every pending entry.\\n\\n```text\\nDaily Builder: 35 -> 45/128 (+10) | Community Weekly Build: 40 -> 50/5000 (+10)\\n```\\n\\nChat batching is enabled by default and can be overridden globally, per event, or per goal.\\n\\n## Platform compatibility\\n\\nThe release jar uses the shared Java 8 platform layer and selects its backend at runtime. Spigot receives legacy-compatible messages, Paper uses native Adventure where available, and Folia uses entity, region, global, and asynchronous schedulers according to the operation.\\n\\nChat messages, action bars, titles, subtitles, and MiniMessage formatting therefore use one API across the supported server platforms.\\n\\n## Integration notes\\n\\nThe Maven API version is now `beta-26.1`. Existing integrations can continue using the historical `com.dadaachievements.api` package and `DadaAchievementsApi` service name.\\n\\nInstall only `DadaProgressions-beta-26.1.jar` on the server. The API artifact belongs on an integration plugin\'s compile classpath with `provided` scope and must not be installed as a standalone plugin."}]}}')}}]);